{
  "title": "Evaluate replacing gpt-4o-mini as the default AI model",
  "description": "The default model in `ramekin-core/src/ai/config.rs` is \
still `openai/gpt-4o-mini`. That model is now ~1.5 years old and has been \
surpassed on quality *and* price by several drop-in alternatives available on \
OpenRouter:\n\
\n\
- `openai/gpt-4.1-mini` / `openai/gpt-4.1-nano` — OpenAI's successor \
small-model line, better instruction-following and similar/cheaper pricing.\n\
- `anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5` — small fast Claude, very strong at short \
classification/rewriting tasks with JSON output.\n\
- `google/gemini-2.5-flash` — cheapest of the set, strong structured-output \
performance.\n\
- `deepseek/deepseek-chat-v3` — very cheap, competitive on reasoning.\n\
\n\
All of our AI call sites (auto_tag, normalize_title, custom_enrich, \
photo_extract) request small JSON outputs and benefit from better instruction \
adherence rather than raw horsepower, so a stronger small model is a win \
across the board.\n\
\n\
Tasks:\n\
- Pick 2-3 candidate models and benchmark them against our existing cached \
prompts. The `normalize_title` paprika test file \
(`data/title-normalization.txt`) is a good candidate for A/B comparison since \
it already has ~500 fixtures and a known \"better\" target behavior.\n\
- Measure cost/latency per request vs. gpt-4o-mini.\n\
- Update `DEFAULT_MODEL` in `config.rs` if a clear winner emerges.\n\
- Note: changing the default will blow away the disk cache (cache path \
includes the model name), so we'll regenerate `data/title-normalization.txt` \
and re-run `make pipeline` as part of the switch.",
